## Bug-Introducing Commits
BIC: 5be3447e77d228b4db8755bfdbcdb0844a756625

## Reasoning

**(1) Investigation:** The fix commit (16abeae) changes `int pageNum = 0;` to `int pageNum = -1;` inside `BookView.getPageNumberFor()`. I used `git log -p -L` on that line range and `git log -S"int pageNum = 0"` to trace the history of that exact statement, finding only two commits ever touched it: `dd726b8` (a bulk file-move/directory-restructure commit that merely re-created the file verbatim — not a real logic change) and `16abeae` (the fix itself). Since the literal initializer never changed in between, the bug had to come from a change in the surrounding loop logic that made `pageNum` start one too high relative to the rest of the arithmetic.

**(2) The faulty change:** Commit `5be3447e` refactored `getPageNumberFor`. Before it, the method branched:
```java
if (strategy.isScrolling()) {
    offsets = pageOffsets.get(index);
    for (i=0; i < offsets.size() && offsets.get(i) < position; i++) pageNum++;
} else {
    pageNum += ((FixedPagesStrategy) strategy).getCurrentPage();
}
```
`5be3447e` collapsed this into a single unconditional loop and, critically, changed the comparison operator from `<` to `<=`:
```java
for (i=0; i < offsets.size() && offsets.get(i) <= position; i++) pageNum++;
```
This means whenever `position` exactly equals a page-offset boundary, the loop now counts one extra page compared to before, systematically off-by-one overcounting `pageNum`. The later fix (`16abeae`) compensates for exactly this by starting `pageNum` at `-1` instead of `0`, which only cancels out the extra increment introduced by `<=`.

**(3) Supporting evidence:** I walked every commit between `5be3447e` and `16abeae` that touched `BookView.java` (`680c485`, `5a8a9c9`, and a dozen others) and none of them modified `getPageNumberFor`'s loop or comparison logic — only unrelated null-checks and logging were added. This confirms the `<=` off-by-one introduced in `5be3447e` is what the eventual `-1` initialization in `16abeae` was specifically patching.

**Uncertainty:** I'm fairly confident in this identification because the fix's arithmetic (decrementing the base by exactly 1) lines up precisely with the semantic effect of switching `<` to `<=` in the loop condition, and no other commit in the intervening history touched this method. However, I cannot run the test suite or reproduce the actual off-by-one numerically (no runtime available in this read-only investigation), so this conclusion is based on code-reading/logical inference rather than empirical confirmation.

